Ladybird now fires a mouse button’s click, auxclick, and contextmenu
events as PointerEvents, and so do the other engines: Blink creates them
in MouseEventManager::DispatchMouseEvent(), and WebKit in its Element::
dispatchMouseEvent(). Gecko sends them as WidgetPointerEvents, with the
messages ePointerClick and ePointerAuxClick, which EventDispatcher::
CreateEvent() turns into DOM PointerEvents.

Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5, all three
fire all three events as PointerEvents with pointerType mouse. Firefox
and WebKit set isPrimary on them, as Ladybird does; Chrome doesn’t. A
contextmenu’s detail is 0 in Chrome and WebKit, as in Ladybird, and 1
in Firefox. And on an embedded YouTube player, a click on the progress
bar seeks, and a click on the video pauses and resumes it, in Chrome
and in Ladybird with this change — but not in Ladybird without it.
